+      "title": "Deterministic noninjectivity in complex dimensions two and three",
+      "statement": "Every complex frame with M=2,N=3 or M=3,N=7 fails phaseless injectivity modulo global phase. If the vectors span, a nonzero Hermitian kernel matrix cannot be semidefinite. In dimension two a nonzero kernel matrix exists and has rank two. In dimension three a two-dimensional kernel contains a nonzero singular matrix by the odd cubic determinant and the intermediate value theorem; this matrix is indefinite of rank two. If the vectors do not span, a nonzero blind vector already gives failure. Thus p_2=p_3=0.",
+      "proof_text": "# P0015: a nonempty open set of failing frames for every M>=2\n\nStatus: complete proof of the first clause submitted to w346; no KEEP yet, and no open problem is reported solved. The asymptotic question p_M -> 0 remains unresolved here. No novelty claim.\n\nFor every M>=2, among complex frames with N=4M-5 measurement vectors there is a nonempty open set that fails phaseless injectivity modulo global phase. A Gaussian frame has positive probability of belonging to this set. Consequently p_M<1, subject to acceptance of this proof. In addition p_2=p_3=0, with deterministic obstructions for every frame.\n\n## Explicit transverse failing frame\n\nPut H_0=diag(1,-1,0,...,0). Use the following N vectors:\n\n    e_1+e_2, e_1-e_2, e_1+i e_2;\n    e_1+e_2+e_j, e_1+e_2+i e_j,\n    e_1-e_2+e_j, e_1-e_2+i e_j   for each j=3,...,M.\n\nEvery vector a has a^* H_0 a=0. They span C^M.\n\nA real-analytic chart of rank-two Hermitian matrices near H_0, with H_11 fixed at one, is\n\n    H(A,B) = [[A, B], [B^*, B^* A^{-1} B]],\n    A=[[1,z],[conjugate(z),beta]], beta real, z complex,\n    B an arbitrary complex 2-by-(M-2) matrix.\n\nHere A is invertible with one positive and one negative eigenvalue near diag(1,-1). By its zero Schur complement, H has rank exactly two and the same nonzero inertia. The chart has 3+4(M-2)=N real parameters.\n\nLet G_i(a,A,B)=a_i^* H(A,B)a_i. At H_0 the derivative of the bottom-right block with respect to chart parameters is zero. In the first three rows the derivative in (beta,Re z,Im z) is\n\n    [[1,2,0],[1,-2,0],[1,0,-2]],\n\nwhich has determinant eight. For each j, subtract the derivative of e_1+e_2 or e_1-e_2 from its corresponding extra row. The remaining four rows, in (Re B_1j,Im B_1j,Re B_2j,Im B_2j), are\n\n    [[2,0,2,0],[0,-2,0,-2],[2,0,-2,0],[0,-2,0,2]],\n\nwhich has determinant 64. The full chart derivative is therefore invertible, with determinant 8*64^{M-2} in this ordering.\n\nThe real implicit function theorem provides, for every frame sufficiently close to this explicit frame, a chart point with all N equations G_i=0. Its matrix H remains nonzero, rank two and indefinite. Its spectral decomposition is H=xx^*-yy^* with noncollinear nonzero x,y. Thus |a_i^*x|^2=|a_i^*y|^2 for every i, while x and y are not equivalent under global phase. This proves an open set of noninjective frames. The Gaussian law on C^{N M} has a strictly positive density everywhere, so this open set has positive probability. No quantitative lower bound uniform in M follows.\n\n## Deterministic low-dimensional cases\n\nIf the measurement vectors do not span C^M, a nonzero blind vector and zero give the same measurements. Otherwise a nonzero positive or negative semidefinite Hermitian matrix cannot lie in the measurement kernel: a_i^* H a_i=0 for a spanning set would force its square root, and hence H, to vanish.\n\nFor M=2,N=3, the real dimension of the Hermitian space is four, so the kernel contains a nonzero matrix. It is indefinite and rank two, giving the ambiguity above.\n\nFor M=3,N=7, the Hermitian space has real dimension nine, so the kernel contains linearly independent H,K. Along the nonzero circle cos(t)H+sin(t)K, the determinant is continuous and changes sign under t -> t+pi, being homogeneous of odd degree three. The intermediate value theorem gives a nonzero singular kernel matrix. It cannot be semidefinite; hence it has rank two and one eigenvalue of each sign. This gives a phaseless ambiguity for every spanning frame too.\n\nSource and continuation: P0015-A001 established M=2 only. This extends it to M=3 and proves the positive-probability failure clause by an explicit invertible Jacobian for all M. The cached Randomstrasse conjecture's separate asymptotic clause is untouched.\n",
